PLACEHOLDER — replace this file before publishing anything.

===============================================================================
The Rust convention: dual MIT OR Apache-2.0
===============================================================================

The overwhelming majority of the Rust ecosystem, including the compiler and the
standard library, is licensed:

    MIT OR Apache-2.0

The `OR` is a genuine choice offered to the user, not a requirement to comply
with both. It is SPDX syntax, and it is what belongs in `Cargo.toml`:

    [workspace.package]
    license = "MIT OR Apache-2.0"

Why both, rather than either alone:

  * Apache-2.0 includes an express patent grant. MIT does not, which leaves
    patent rights to be inferred, and some corporate legal reviews will not
    accept that.

  * Apache-2.0 is incompatible with GPLv2. MIT is not. Offering both means a
    GPLv2 project can use your crate under MIT while a company that requires a
    patent grant can use it under Apache-2.0.

  * Because it is what the ecosystem already does, `cargo deny`'s allow-lists,
    corporate approval processes and licence scanners are all configured for
    it. Choosing something else means every downstream consumer has a
    conversation about you.

===============================================================================
What to do instead of shipping this file
===============================================================================

Convention is two files at the repository root, both referenced from the
README:

    LICENSE-MIT          the MIT licence text
    LICENSE-APACHE       the Apache License 2.0 text

Get the texts from https://spdx.org/licenses/ or from any established Rust
project. Do not retype them; the text is the licence.

Then add the standard contribution notice to CONTRIBUTING.md — see the
"Licence" section there.

If you keep a single `LICENSE` file instead, make sure it holds the actual
licence text and that `license` in `Cargo.toml` matches it. A `license` field
that disagrees with the file in the repository is worse than either alone.

Other reasonable choices, and when:

  * MIT alone            — maximum simplicity, no patent grant
  * Apache-2.0 alone     — patent grant, GPLv2-incompatible
  * MPL-2.0              — file-level copyleft
  * AGPL-3.0 / GPL-3.0   — strong copyleft; expect near-zero adoption as a
                           library, since it propagates to every consumer
  * Proprietary          — set `publish = false` in every `[package]` and use
                           `license-file` or an explicit SPDX expression

Whatever you pick, say so in `Cargo.toml`. An unlicensed crate is legally
unusable by everyone, including the people you wanted to use it.
